a series of higher lows since the 183.64 trough on 5 July, and the last seven closes have all held comfortably above the 185.85 prior reaction zone. The broader structure remains tilted in favour of the euro against the yen.\n\nThe structure story over the past month is one of patient accumulation followed by a decisive leg higher. After oscillating in the 184.20\u2013185.90 band through most of July, the pair broke clear of that congestion on 21 July and has since posted consecutive daily closes above 186.00. The 185.99\u2013186.04 area, which capped multiple intraday attempts in late July, has now flipped in character and sits as the most immediately relevant structural floor. Beneath that, the desk notes the 185.85 prior reaction zone, which attracted buyers on at least three separate occasions during the mid-July consolidation, and the 185.25 structure support as the level that would materially challenge the bullish read if revisited. The 186.75 area, where sellers stepped in on both 27 and 26 July, represents the near-term ceiling the bulls need to clear to extend the advance.\n\nOn the hourly chart, the picture is one of orderly digestion. From the 186.75 session high on Monday, price drifted lower through the overnight session, finding a floor around 186.04\u2013186.13 during the early Asian hours \u2014 a zone that corresponds neatly with the prior reaction support identified on the daily. Since then, the H1 has been grinding back toward the mid-186.20s, with each hourly close holding above the 186.21 prior reaction zone. The recovery from the overnight low is measured rather than impulsive, which is consistent with consolidation ahead of a potential continuation rather than a reversal. Momentum on the shorter timeframe appears to be rebuilding quietly.\n\nToday's calendar is notably light for both EUR and JPY. The BOJ Core CPI print at 01:00 UTC has already passed, and the Spanish Unemployment Rate and German Bundesbank Monthly Report carry low-impact designations. Absent a meaningful surprise in any of these releases, the session is unlikely to see event-driven volatility, which could allow technical structure to dominate price action more cleanly than on busier macro days.\n\nWhat would alter the bullish read? A sustained hourly close back beneath the 186.04 prior reaction zone would introduce caution, and a daily close beneath 185.85 would meaningfully undermine the recent higher-low sequence. Should the 185.25 structure support give way on a closing basis, the near-term bullish thesis would require reassessment in full.\n\nThis is editorial analysis, not financial advice. Trading involves substantial risk of loss.", "sample": false}